WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:02.360
They say "all you need is love."

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:04.960
They also say "age ain't nothin' but a number."

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.320
But every so often when you're watching a
movie, you see a mismatched couple — or

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.240
lovestruck criminal — taking things too
far.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:14.780
Here are some of the most uncomfortable age
gaps in movies.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.440
That's My Boy

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:21.220
Even if you're a huge Adam Sandler fan, you've
got to admit that he didn't just cross a line

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.760
in That's My Boy—he straight-up pole vaulted
over that sucker.

00:00:25.009 --> 00:00:29.840
The premise of this 2012 comedy is utterly
tasteless, as it involves a tween version

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:32.300
of Sandler's character having sex with his
teacher.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:37.760
Just to be clear, we're talking about a 12-year-old
boy being coerced into having sex with an

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:41.380
adult, resulting in a pregnancy, all played
as a joke.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:46.660
Considering That's My Boy only has a 20 percent
on Rotten Tomatoes and totally flopped at

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:50.500
the box office, it seems like the joke's on
Sandler and his crude crew.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:51.900
Big

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:58.120
The 1988 comedy classic Big follows a kid
named Josh who magically morphs into a 30-year-old

00:00:58.120 --> 00:00:58.620
man.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:03.960
At first, this transformation seems pretty
cool, especially when Josh is paid big bucks

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.540
to play with toys for a living.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:09.800
But things get problematic when he starts
dating Susan, his adult coworker.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:11.040
"It's for you.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.700
It's a glow-in-the-dark compass ring, so you
won't get lost."

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:20.340
True, Tom Hanks and Elizabeth Perkins are
about the same age in real life, but in the

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.560
movie, Josh is actually only 12.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.880
So when the two end up sleeping together,
we're witnessing a serious felony offense,

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:31.159
even though Susan doesn't yet know about Josh's
condition.

00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:34.880
It's hard to wrap your head around, true,
but it's undeniably creepy at the end when

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:40.229
Josh reverts to his child form, and grown
woman Susan waves goodbye to her 12-year-old

00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:41.240
ex-boyfriend.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:43.120
American Beauty

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:48.500
Kevin Spacey won the Best Actor Oscar for
playing Lester Burnham in American Beauty,

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:52.951
a middle-aged loser who's inspired to take
control of his life after falling for a high

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:53.800
school student.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:57.700
Making things even worse, she's his daughter's
best friend, Angela.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:02.480
Roger Ebert noted correctly that the film
wasn't really about a "Lolita relationship,"

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:07.520
but instead about "yearning after youth, respect,
power, and, of course, beauty."

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:11.989
But Lester's fantasies about bedding a child
are revolting — you can't help but agree

00:02:11.989 --> 00:02:16.629
with actress Kirsten Dunst, who had the good
sense to turn down the role of Angela because

00:02:16.629 --> 00:02:22.409
she, quote, "didn't want to kiss Kevin Spacey
or be seen lying naked in those rose petals."

00:02:22.409 --> 00:02:24.420
After all, she was only 15.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.420
Leon: The Professional

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:32.300
French director Luc Besson's 1994 action flick 
Leon: The Professional is a movie that'll

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.100
leave you feeling a little odd.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.800
While the relationship between Jean Reno's
Leon and Natalie Portman's Mathilda isn't

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.660
"inappropriate" per se, it is just a tad…disconcerting.

00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:48.159
The plot involves a 12-year-old girl who asks
a hitman to get revenge on the psychotic DEA

00:02:48.159 --> 00:02:49.659
agent who murdered her family.

00:02:49.659 --> 00:02:55.000
Leon and Mathilda's relationship starts off
strange—he's childlike, she's wise for her

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.530
years—and only gets weirder from there ...

00:02:57.980 --> 00:02:59.160
"I love you, Mathilda."

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.219
"I love you, too, Leon."

00:03:01.219 --> 00:03:05.079
… especially when she starts crushing on
Leon and telling strangers this full-grown

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:09.100
man is her lover … despite Leon always keeping
things professional.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:11.700
The Diary of a Teenage Girl

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:16.219
Based on the acclaimed graphic novel, The
Diary of a Teenage Girl is meant to push boundaries

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:17.900
and start conversations.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:23.420
After all, this is a film about a 15-year-old
girl discovering her sexuality, a subject

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:25.639
that's rarely discussed on the big screen.

00:03:25.639 --> 00:03:30.019
However, some might argue that Diary takes
things a bit far when its teenage protagonist

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:32.760
winds up in bed with a 35-year-old man.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:38.300
The 15-year-old in question is played by Bel
Powley, who was actually in her 20s while

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:38.800
filming.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:45.220
She's a wannabe cartoonist in 1976 who loses
her virginity to her mom's boyfriend, Monroe,

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:46.960
played by Alexander Skarsgard.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:52.440
As critic Kate Erbland points out, this is
a movie about a girl in control and "making

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:56.529
her own decisions, even if they're probably
bad ones" — and watching the consequences

00:03:56.529 --> 00:04:01.040
of those bad decisions — however poignantly
they're portrayed — will likely make you

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:01.540
squirm.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:03.180
Harold and Maude

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:08.300
Perhaps the most famous movie ever made about
May/December relationships, Harold and Maude

00:04:08.309 --> 00:04:13.499
was a box office failure in 1971, but over
the years, it's gained a cult following.

00:04:13.499 --> 00:04:18.239
Today, it's often considered must-watch cinema,
although if you're only familiar with the

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:22.810
basic premise, you might think this is a film
simply about a kid who sleeps with a woman

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:24.420
old enough to be his grandma.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:29.240
Of course, Harold and Maude isn't just about
the sex — we don't even see anything happen

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:29.920
onscreen.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:34.940
The film follows death-obsessed Harold, a
20-year-old who fakes suicide and visits random

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:35.620
funerals.

00:04:35.949 --> 00:04:41.200
Eventually, he runs into the larger-than-life
Maude, a 79-year-old who teaches Harold how

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:42.200
to really live ...

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:43.880
"Your license, lady?"

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:45.580
"I don't have one.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:46.080
I don't believe in them."

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:47.960
Manhattan

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:53.560
1979's Manhattan is widely considered to be
one of the greatest movies ever made.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:58.020
But it's also a film about Woody Allen having
a fling with a 17-year-old.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:03.300
Allen plays a 40-something comedy writer that
has no qualms being involved with a high school

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:08.120
girl played by Mariel Hemingway—he even
finds it amusing when she ends a date because

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:09.659
she has to study for a test.

00:05:09.659 --> 00:05:11.189
"But you're 17 years old.

00:05:11.189 --> 00:05:16.550
By the time you're 21, you're gonna have,  you'll have a dozen
relationships — believe me — far more

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:17.810
passionate than this one."

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:22.440
The events of the film followed Hemingway
into real life: the actress says after she

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:27.389
turned 18, Allen showed up at her house and
allegedly asked her parents to let her accompany

00:05:27.389 --> 00:05:28.600
him to Paris.

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:29.600
She said no.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:34.610
In the late-1980s, Allen began a relationship
with his long time girlfriend Mia Farrow's

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:38.320
adopted daughter, Soon-Yi Previn, who later
became his wife..

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:39.720
Lolita

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:45.319
Lolita is often hailed as one of the best
novels of the 20th century, but it's not exactly

00:05:45.319 --> 00:05:46.720
what you'd call light reading.

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:53.090
Nevertheless, the dark and twisted novel inspired
two separate movies, Stanley Kubrick's 1962

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:59.560
version starring James Mason and Sue Lyon,
and Adrian Lyne's 1997 version featuring Jeremy

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:01.380
Irons and Dominique Swain.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:07.440
Both movies focus on Humbert Humbert, a self-proclaimed
"nymphophile" who is only sexually attracted

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:11.300
to post-pubescent teen girls who meet his
specific criteria.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:17.159
Both Lyon and Swain were actually 14 and 15,
respectively, so naturally, displaying the

00:06:17.159 --> 00:06:20.599
subject matter on screen stirred up a lot
of controversy.

00:06:20.599 --> 00:06:25.969
Kubrick's version is far tamer than Lyne's,
but it still made a lot of people uncomfortable,

00:06:25.969 --> 00:06:27.820
prompting the now-classic question …

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:30.600
"How did they ever make a movie of Lolita?"

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:32.240
Thanks for watching!

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:35.289
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:35.289 --> 00:06:37.650
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

